WEBVTT

1
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:09,160
<i>But what would happen if all
these neutral nations I have mentioned,</i>

2
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:12,120
<i>and some others I have not mentioned,</i>

3
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:16,520
<i>were with one spontaneous impulse
to do their duty,</i>

4
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:21,080
<i>and were to stand together
with the British and French empires,</i>

5
00:01:21,240 --> 00:01:23,280
<i>against aggression and wrong?</i>

6
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:26,360
<i>At present, their plight is lamentable,</i>

7
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:29,960
<i>and it will become much worse.</i>

8
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:34,880
<i>Each one hopes
that if he feeds the crocodile enough,</i>

9
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:37,560
<i>the crocodile will eat him last.</i>

10
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:41,520
<i>All of them hope
that the storm will pass</i>

11
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:44,680
<i>before their turn comes to be devoured.</i>

12
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:46,680
<i>But I fear...</i>

13
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:48,400
<i>I fear greatly</i>

14
00:01:48,560 --> 00:01:50,160
<i>that storm will not pass.</i>

15
00:01:51,240 --> 00:01:53,840
<i>It will rage and it will roar,</i>

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,640
<i>ever more loudly, ever more widely.</i>

17
00:01:57,800 --> 00:01:59,760
<i>It will spread to the south,</i>

18
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:02,000
<i>it will spread to the north...</i>

19
00:03:00,200 --> 00:03:02,240
- How many?
- Seven.

20
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:04,080
- All last night?
- Yes.

21
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:05,560
None with ID papers?

22
00:03:06,160 --> 00:03:07,080
No.

23
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:09,240
No doubt about it,
send them back.

24
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:24,360
Your parents know you're not allowed
to cross the border without papers.

25
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:47,400
Returning...

26
00:03:50,520 --> 00:03:52,200
Get in.
Back to Germany, come on.

27
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:54,960
Back to Germany.
Come on, hurry up.

28
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:57,800
Quickly. Faster.

29
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:29,960
<i>Tomorrow, May 6th,
the sun won't be shining in Antwerp.</i>

30
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:31,360
<i>But no rain is expected.</i>

31
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:33,440
<i>Temperatures will reach 16°C,</i>

32
00:04:33,600 --> 00:04:35,200
<i>time to plant those flower bulbs.</i>

33
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:39,040
<i>There's also
happy news from the zoo!</i>

34
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:42,680
<i>A cub has been born
in the lion enclosure!</i>

35
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:44,640
When are you leaving?

36
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:46,200
We're not.

37
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:48,040
We're staying.

38
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:50,720
My father's changed his mind.

39
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:52,600
We're up again.

40
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:55,920
One o'clock. Finor time.

41
00:04:56,840 --> 00:04:57,760
Thank you.

42
00:04:58,440 --> 00:05:01,760
<i>I'll be back after 1 o'clock
with news from the Almanac.</i>

43
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:02,880
<i>Until then.</i>

44
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:19,760
<i>Oh Finor, oh Finor,</i>

45
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:22,440
<i>A bar every day</i>

46
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:27,320
<i>Oh Finor, oh Finor,</i>

47
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:30,160
<i>A bite, oh yes, please</i>

48
00:05:31,440 --> 00:05:34,920
<i>Chocolate full of flavour</i>

49
00:05:35,400 --> 00:05:38,760
<i>Early in the morning
And on, throughout the day</i>

50
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:42,040
<i>Finor chocolate</i>

51
00:05:42,760 --> 00:05:44,760
<i>A bite, full of happiness.</i>

52
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:05,960
Five francs an hour.
I was here four hours, that's 20 francs.

53
00:06:06,120 --> 00:06:08,760
A box of Finor is worth
more than 20 francs.

54
00:06:09,520 --> 00:06:11,600
Chocolate to buy
my ticket to Brussels?

55
00:06:11,760 --> 00:06:13,520
Why was it again
your father moved to Brussels?

56
00:06:18,720 --> 00:06:19,680
Five francs.

57
00:06:24,480 --> 00:06:25,520
The door!

58
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:37,600
<i>"I consider myself fortunate
that in my misfortune,</i>

59
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:40,360
<i>"I have a noble and honourable
knight as my guide.</i>

60
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:45,320
<i>"But what if, on my journey,
I were escorted by common soldiers?"</i>

61
00:07:57,600 --> 00:07:59,320
Well, if it isn't our Berre!

62
00:07:59,480 --> 00:08:02,000
- Aunt Gabby.
- My lad!

63
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:05,640
You smell of Limburg.
Am I wrong?

64
00:08:06,280 --> 00:08:07,560
How was the journey?

65
00:08:07,720 --> 00:08:09,480
How's my sister doing? Well?

66
00:08:10,040 --> 00:08:11,080
Come in.

67
00:08:11,720 --> 00:08:13,440
Uncle André is still at work.

68
00:08:47,080 --> 00:08:48,640
- Hello.
- Hello.

69
00:08:54,200 --> 00:08:57,560
Payment in kind.
They've run out of money.

70
00:08:57,720 --> 00:08:59,000
I'm not going back.

71
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:01,600
Good.

72
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:03,440
It's about time we left here.

73
00:09:18,840 --> 00:09:20,640
What did you have to do for this?

74
00:09:21,440 --> 00:09:23,120
Here. Read.

75
00:09:26,120 --> 00:09:28,680
"Joan of Navarre is an evil queen.

76
00:09:29,640 --> 00:09:32,280
She has imprisoned
my father and my relatives."

77
00:09:33,200 --> 00:09:34,880
"It is true, noble lady,

78
00:09:35,040 --> 00:09:37,360
our queen treats
the Flemish very harshly."

79
00:09:37,720 --> 00:09:39,440
When is your exam?

80
00:09:39,600 --> 00:09:42,080
No exam.
A voice test. Tomorrow.

81
00:09:43,280 --> 00:09:46,040
"I consider myself fortunate
that in my misfortune,

82
00:09:46,200 --> 00:09:48,400
"I have an honourable,
loyal knight as my guide.

83
00:09:48,560 --> 00:09:50,960
"But what if, on my journey,

84
00:09:51,120 --> 00:09:53,200
"I were escorted
by common soldiers?"

85
00:09:59,040 --> 00:10:00,080
So, that's him?

86
00:10:00,240 --> 00:10:02,040
He got here an hour ago.

87
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:03,840
I almost didn't recognise him.

88
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:05,920
Last time I saw him,
he was just a little boy.

89
00:10:07,160 --> 00:10:10,520
He didn't dare give me a kiss.
I think he's a little embarrassed.

90
00:10:11,040 --> 00:10:14,640
He used all his equipment
to record the sound of our doorbell.

91
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:16,760
Hello there, Berre.

92
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:24,560
This is Uncle André,
my new husband.

93
00:10:24,720 --> 00:10:26,160
- Thank you.
- What for?

94
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:27,520
The internship.

95
00:10:27,800 --> 00:10:29,120
Oh, that was Gabby's idea.

96
00:10:30,040 --> 00:10:31,280
Your mum told me

97
00:10:31,440 --> 00:10:35,160
how much you like playing around
with microphones and all that.

98
00:10:35,720 --> 00:10:38,760
I thought: "Perfect,
André works at the radio station, so..."

99
00:10:46,720 --> 00:10:50,080
Maybe you can take
his equipment upstairs, André.

100
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,720
- There we go.
- You go first.

101
00:11:01,880 --> 00:11:03,520
It's all going up to the attic.

102
00:11:20,840 --> 00:11:23,160
- What have you got in here?
- Sounds.

103
00:11:24,520 --> 00:11:26,280
I didn't know sound was so heavy.

104
00:11:27,360 --> 00:11:29,040
A few rumours in there too.

105
00:11:43,760 --> 00:11:45,800
<i>In a few moments,
"The Politics Chronicle"</i>

106
00:11:45,960 --> 00:11:48,080
<i>by Mr Fleischman, with the latest news</i>

107
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:51,920
<i>concerning the German-Austrian threat</i>

108
00:11:52,080 --> 00:11:53,760
<i>in the face of reinforced...</i>

109
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,320
<i>Live from the Flagey building
in Brussels</i>

110
00:11:59,480 --> 00:12:01,480
<i>the Drama Company presents</i>

111
00:12:01,640 --> 00:12:03,760
<i>the radio play:
Arsenic and Apple Tart.</i>

112
00:12:03,920 --> 00:12:05,800
So, you think he's a little shy?

113
00:12:05,960 --> 00:12:08,280
Let the boy listen to his programme.

114
00:12:08,440 --> 00:12:09,680
And my programme?

115
00:12:09,840 --> 00:12:13,760
<i>When Hilter invades, there'll be
plenty of time to listen to that drivel.</i>

116
00:12:13,920 --> 00:12:15,840
<i>- Hitler.
- Hilter, that's what I said.</i>

117
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:19,120
<i>Sound effects, Pierre Polak.</i>

118
00:12:19,280 --> 00:12:21,560
Ah, Polak, your internship is with him.

119
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:24,160
It's started. Eat your soup.

120
00:12:38,720 --> 00:12:41,520
<i>What aromas are these?
Freshly ground coffee.</i>

121
00:12:41,680 --> 00:12:43,600
<i>Unusual smells for a pharmacy.</i>

122
00:12:43,760 --> 00:12:45,760
<i>Hello sir, welcome in our shop.</i>

123
00:12:46,040 --> 00:12:48,000
<i>How can I help you?</i>

124
00:12:48,160 --> 00:12:51,240
<i>I am Inspector Nose.
Could you call the pharmacist for me?</i>

125
00:12:51,400 --> 00:12:53,640
<i>He is currently unavailable.</i>

126
00:12:53,800 --> 00:12:56,040
<i>I very much doubt that, miss.</i>

127
00:12:56,200 --> 00:13:00,600
<i>At this very moment, he is washing down
a slice of freshly baked tart</i>

128
00:13:00,760 --> 00:13:01,960
<i>with a gulp of hot coffee.</i>

129
00:13:02,240 --> 00:13:03,880
<i>How do you know that, inspector?</i>

130
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:06,600
<i>Dear lady, my nose never fails me.</i>

131
00:13:06,760 --> 00:13:08,240
<i>Unlike in matters of love,</i>

132
00:13:08,400 --> 00:13:10,960
<i>my moustachioed friend
has never let me down.</i>

133
00:13:11,120 --> 00:13:14,120
<i>Nicodemus Nose, my trusted friend...</i>

134
00:13:20,720 --> 00:13:23,000
As a little boy,
I dreamt of working on a ship.

135
00:13:23,160 --> 00:13:24,320
And look at me now...

136
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:26,520
The Flagey building.

137
00:13:26,960 --> 00:13:29,360
The world's largest sound factory.

138
00:13:30,200 --> 00:13:33,720
On this ship, I'm not "Uncle André",
they call me "Chasseur André".

139
00:13:33,880 --> 00:13:34,800
Understood?

140
00:13:37,400 --> 00:13:40,600
For someone who likes to make noise,
you're pretty quiet.

141
00:14:06,080 --> 00:14:10,120
There are six studios for concerts,
five for the news and two for records.

142
00:14:10,280 --> 00:14:13,080
And four for radio plays,
where your internship is.

143
00:14:13,360 --> 00:14:16,560
And we're the only place in the world
with two echo chambers

144
00:14:16,880 --> 00:14:19,360
where sounds are trapped
and can't escape.

145
00:14:19,520 --> 00:14:20,520
Sign here, please.

146
00:14:23,800 --> 00:14:26,520
Also, we have three directors.

147
00:14:27,280 --> 00:14:29,360
Who else has three directors?

148
00:14:29,520 --> 00:14:31,840
One for French speakers,
one for the Flemish,

149
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,400
and one for both
French and Flemish speakers.

150
00:14:34,560 --> 00:14:36,560
What a wonderful country we live in.

151
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:44,600
Dear colleagues,

152
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:47,080
this is Berre, my wife's nephew.

153
00:14:48,640 --> 00:14:50,960
He's Polak's 'sound effects' intern.

154
00:14:51,120 --> 00:14:52,360
- Foley.
- What's that?

155
00:14:52,520 --> 00:14:54,040
It's called 'Foley' nowadays.

156
00:14:58,200 --> 00:15:03,480
<i>And is it not important that creation
around us be not only useful,</i>

157
00:15:03,640 --> 00:15:07,840
<i>and that its usefulness speak to us
less than its beauty...</i>

158
00:15:08,800 --> 00:15:13,560
<i>And that it be the beauty of sounds
that speak to us most.</i>

159
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:20,160
Don't worry,
Director Van Rooy and I are ready.

160
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:24,000
If the Germans invade, we will evacuate
the building within two hours.

161
00:15:24,160 --> 00:15:27,000
All the Nazis will find
is an empty box.

162
00:15:27,480 --> 00:15:28,840
One last thing...

163
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,640
Where are we
with the 'Lists of Suspects'?

164
00:15:31,800 --> 00:15:34,360
The Minister of Justice
stresses the urgency.

165
00:15:34,520 --> 00:15:36,360
Last month's Minister or today's?

166
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:37,480
Dear colleague...

167
00:15:37,640 --> 00:15:40,640
How many Ministers of Justice
have we had in 18 months?

168
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:43,880
- Five... or is it six?
- These are confusing times...

169
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:45,560
As is the minister's request:

170
00:15:45,720 --> 00:15:47,840
"Draw up lists of those we suspect

171
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:51,280
would help the Germans
in the event of a German invasion."

172
00:15:51,440 --> 00:15:54,240
Indeed: fascists, rexists,
Flemish nationalists.

173
00:15:54,400 --> 00:15:58,080
But also spies hidden among all
the refugees from Germany and Austria.

174
00:15:58,240 --> 00:16:02,160
Individuals who may belong
to the Fifth Column.

175
00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:05,360
- Not that complicated, is it?
- Spies from the Fifth Column?

176
00:16:05,640 --> 00:16:08,240
We have 644 employees.

177
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:11,040
Do some of them
have Nazi sympathies?

178
00:16:11,200 --> 00:16:12,600
Yes, maybe.

179
00:16:12,760 --> 00:16:16,000
But how are we to determine that?
It's not written on their foreheads.

180
00:16:16,280 --> 00:16:19,560
As long as they don't walk in here
in uniform, there's little we can do.

181
00:16:20,880 --> 00:16:23,960
You know we uphold
the neutrality of public broadcasting.

182
00:16:24,120 --> 00:16:27,120
But the question's vagueness
may lead to arbitrariness

183
00:16:27,280 --> 00:16:29,640
and, above all,
lead to false accusations.

184
00:16:29,800 --> 00:16:32,360
Listen, we'll keep a close eye
on the situation.

185
00:16:32,520 --> 00:16:34,880
Our staff know
they can tell the secretariat

186
00:16:35,040 --> 00:16:36,960
if they notice anything suspicious.

187
00:16:42,440 --> 00:16:44,640
Stand up straight and avoid eye contact.

188
00:16:46,240 --> 00:16:47,520
Director...

189
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:49,680
Director.

190
00:16:52,840 --> 00:16:56,120
Am I really hearing
the Soviet Union's national anthem?

191
00:16:57,040 --> 00:16:59,240
Are communists just
given free rein here?

192
00:17:00,280 --> 00:17:02,080
How neutral is that?

193
00:17:02,240 --> 00:17:05,440
The mornings are set aside
for political broadcasters.

194
00:17:05,600 --> 00:17:06,760
We have no say in that.

195
00:17:06,920 --> 00:17:09,000
It was a political decision.

196
00:17:09,160 --> 00:17:12,280
- Who is broadcasting right now?
- The socialists.

197
00:17:14,120 --> 00:17:16,840
Ah, Chasseur André,
could you open this door, please?

198
00:17:23,800 --> 00:17:25,840
They've locked it from the inside.

199
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:29,280
- Can't you block their audio signal?
- Yes, that's possible.

200
00:17:30,960 --> 00:17:34,600
Run faster than 340 metres per second
and you'll outrun the sound wave

201
00:17:34,760 --> 00:17:38,080
and be able to stop it
by standing in front of it.

202
00:17:38,240 --> 00:17:40,320
The wave will
lose its energy and stop.

203
00:17:40,880 --> 00:17:41,880
How fast?

204
00:17:42,040 --> 00:17:44,640
340 metres per second.
The speed of sound.

205
00:17:45,800 --> 00:17:48,280
- That will be difficult.
- It's very fast, yes.

206
00:17:48,440 --> 00:17:50,640
- And you are?
- Berre.

207
00:17:50,800 --> 00:17:53,760
<i>- Uncle André's new wife's nephew.
- Chasseur André.</i>

208
00:17:53,920 --> 00:17:56,000
Apologies.
He doesn't know his place yet.

209
00:17:56,160 --> 00:17:58,920
It's his first day as intern:
radio play sound effects.

210
00:17:59,080 --> 00:18:00,440
With Polak?

211
00:18:04,080 --> 00:18:06,400
- Gentlemen.
- Yes, let's go.

212
00:18:09,480 --> 00:18:11,640
<i>- Chasseur André, I told you.
- Chasseur André.</i>

213
00:18:11,800 --> 00:18:13,160
Come on, hurry up now.

214
00:18:13,720 --> 00:18:15,720
Studio 10.
Pierre Polak.

215
00:18:15,880 --> 00:18:18,240
And don't let me
or your Aunt Gabby down.

216
00:18:35,080 --> 00:18:38,000
I have to report for a voice test
at 10:30.

217
00:18:39,320 --> 00:18:42,880
You were supposed to report
for a voice test at 10:30.

218
00:18:54,880 --> 00:18:56,520
Radio Play, Studio 12,

219
00:18:56,680 --> 00:18:58,960
through the swing doors
and up the staircase.

220
00:18:59,120 --> 00:19:00,240
Thank you.

221
00:19:21,440 --> 00:19:23,160
Sir?

222
00:19:23,320 --> 00:19:24,880
Excuse me, sir. Studio 12?

223
00:19:27,360 --> 00:19:30,520
- You took the Brussels train yesterday?
- Yes.

224
00:19:30,680 --> 00:19:32,800
You were looking to the past,
me to the future.

225
00:19:35,160 --> 00:19:38,760
You faced where you came from,
I prefer looking to where I'm going.

226
00:19:38,920 --> 00:19:40,600
I wasn't looking, but listening.

227
00:19:41,720 --> 00:19:43,120
It makes no difference

228
00:19:43,280 --> 00:19:47,600
on a train which direction you look in,
the sound is exactly the same.

229
00:19:48,280 --> 00:19:49,920
It's all about the energy.

230
00:19:51,680 --> 00:19:55,200
Yes, well, I have a voice test
in the Radio Play studio.

231
00:19:56,960 --> 00:19:58,120
Follow me.

232
00:20:01,640 --> 00:20:05,160
"How does my...
How my heart longs for his kisses.

233
00:20:05,320 --> 00:20:08,720
"His words so soft and...

234
00:20:09,480 --> 00:20:11,840
"sweet..."
Soft and sweet!

235
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,200
"So soft and sweet!"

236
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:21,400
Yes, my apologies,
there may have been a mistake in...

237
00:20:21,560 --> 00:20:23,040
Mr Van Dijck. I'm here.

238
00:20:23,920 --> 00:20:27,360
Miss De Vries. The audition jury was
ready for your mic test at 10:30.

239
00:20:27,520 --> 00:20:30,240
- One person was missing.
- My apologies.

240
00:20:31,120 --> 00:20:33,360
It's well past the academic quarter.

241
00:20:33,520 --> 00:20:37,000
Shame, the other tests went great,
but you've missed your chance.

242
00:20:37,160 --> 00:20:40,920
I've always been on time, but today...
I'd never been in this building...

243
00:20:41,080 --> 00:20:43,240
Young lady, radio does not wait.
Ever.

244
00:20:43,400 --> 00:20:45,200
Radio's more punctual than time.

245
00:20:45,360 --> 00:20:46,720
It's his fault.

246
00:20:47,640 --> 00:20:48,680
And he is?

247
00:20:48,840 --> 00:20:50,320
- Berre.
- Berre.

248
00:20:50,480 --> 00:20:51,400
Berre?

249
00:20:51,800 --> 00:20:52,840
Foley intern.

250
00:20:53,640 --> 00:20:55,000
Sound effects.

251
00:20:55,720 --> 00:20:58,360
We were lost in the building,
through his fault.

252
00:20:58,520 --> 00:20:59,440
Is that so?

253
00:21:00,560 --> 00:21:01,480
Yes.

254
00:21:07,160 --> 00:21:11,000
Jury members, do you agree
to proceed with the studio test?

255
00:21:11,160 --> 00:21:13,880
Thank you for your understanding.
Please follow me.

256
00:21:20,800 --> 00:21:22,040
You talk too loudly.

257
00:21:23,400 --> 00:21:26,640
Studio mics are very sensitive,
so speak more softly.

258
00:21:27,880 --> 00:21:29,160
Soft and sweet.

259
00:21:29,720 --> 00:21:31,440
Yes, soft...

260
00:21:47,160 --> 00:21:48,120
- Ready, Elza?
- Yes.

261
00:21:48,600 --> 00:21:49,520
Lowie?

262
00:21:50,280 --> 00:21:51,760
- Let's get going?
- Yep.

263
00:21:54,160 --> 00:21:55,160
Right.

264
00:21:56,680 --> 00:21:57,840
Concentrate.

265
00:21:58,920 --> 00:22:00,280
Ready, Lowie?

266
00:22:01,120 --> 00:22:03,400
Lowie.
Three, two, one...

267
00:22:03,560 --> 00:22:04,640
Action, Lowie.

268
00:22:05,440 --> 00:22:07,000
Dear Machteld, our suffering

269
00:22:07,160 --> 00:22:09,640
is over:
the fatherland has been avenged,

270
00:22:09,800 --> 00:22:12,520
the lilies have been torn.

271
00:22:12,680 --> 00:22:17,280
Flanders is free!
The French have been defeated.

272
00:22:20,040 --> 00:22:22,040
Dear father.

273
00:22:22,840 --> 00:22:26,400
At last, I see you free,
no longer in chains.

274
00:22:27,320 --> 00:22:29,280
Let me hold you in my arms.

275
00:22:29,800 --> 00:22:31,400
I want to spend
the rest of my life

276
00:22:31,560 --> 00:22:34,600
happy and blessed by your protection!

277
00:22:35,280 --> 00:22:37,320
I never dared hope
of such happiness again.

278
00:22:38,080 --> 00:22:39,000
Dries...

279
00:22:39,800 --> 00:22:42,080
I told you,
I don't want to work with interns.

280
00:22:42,240 --> 00:22:44,280
Mr Polak, we're doing a voice test.

281
00:22:44,440 --> 00:22:46,640
That may be,
but there's some oddball here,

282
00:22:46,920 --> 00:22:48,800
who claims he's working with me.

283
00:22:48,960 --> 00:22:52,200
Pierre, aren't you listening?
We'll discuss it later.

284
00:22:55,560 --> 00:22:58,320
Alright, let's keep going.
Ready...

285
00:22:58,480 --> 00:22:59,800
But father,

286
00:23:00,520 --> 00:23:02,480
why say you nothing of my beloved?

287
00:23:03,560 --> 00:23:05,360
Why is he not here?

288
00:23:05,520 --> 00:23:09,160
How my heart longs for his kisses.
His words so...

289
00:23:10,160 --> 00:23:11,760
soft and sweet.

290
00:23:12,280 --> 00:23:14,360
My happiness was his great dream.

291
00:23:15,320 --> 00:23:18,440
His love was my light,
but now it is extinguished.

292
00:23:53,200 --> 00:23:55,880
BELGIAN DIVING CHAMPIONSHIPS
1913, THIRD PLACE

293
00:24:03,640 --> 00:24:06,640
He sacrificed himself for me
and put on your chains...

294
00:24:07,280 --> 00:24:08,760
Coffee. Ground floor.

295
00:24:09,320 --> 00:24:10,720
He has taken your place.

296
00:24:10,880 --> 00:24:13,840
But how can I go on living,
knowing that he is no longer here?

297
00:24:22,600 --> 00:24:24,840
<i>At 1.30 pm, a first reading.</i>

298
00:24:25,000 --> 00:24:28,440
<i>Rehearsal tomorrow afternoon
and live broadcast tomorrow evening.</i>

299
00:24:28,600 --> 00:24:29,680
Ah, Berre!

300
00:24:29,840 --> 00:24:32,360
Please show Miss De Vries
to the secretariat.

301
00:24:32,800 --> 00:24:35,160
You can get your paperwork done

302
00:24:35,320 --> 00:24:38,040
and you can sign your intern contract.
For Polak?

303
00:24:38,200 --> 00:24:39,280
I'll take it.

304
00:24:40,120 --> 00:24:42,240
- The papers, here.
- Thank you.

305
00:24:42,400 --> 00:24:43,440
Fons.

306
00:24:44,240 --> 00:24:47,360
Elza De Vries. Your new colleague.
Elza, Fons Belloy.

307
00:24:47,520 --> 00:24:49,840
Our star, I think I can say.

308
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:51,400
Mr Belloy, a pleasure.

309
00:24:52,360 --> 00:24:53,520
Miss De Vries.

310
00:24:53,680 --> 00:24:54,840
Elza.

311
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:56,960
She's starting a month's trial,

312
00:24:57,120 --> 00:24:58,640
but she has the qualities

313
00:24:58,800 --> 00:25:00,840
to get a permanent contract
very quickly.

314
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:03,360
And the sound effects intern. Berre.

315
00:25:04,160 --> 00:25:06,360
There you have it.
1.30 pm sharp?

316
00:25:07,120 --> 00:25:08,040
Yes.

317
00:25:11,080 --> 00:25:14,560
- Room 241?
- Yes, 241.

318
00:25:38,240 --> 00:25:39,920
Identity papers.

319
00:25:40,080 --> 00:25:41,520
I don't have them.
I lost them.

320
00:25:42,520 --> 00:25:44,080
I need ID papers to register you.

321
00:25:44,240 --> 00:25:45,520
In-house rules.

322
00:25:45,680 --> 00:25:47,600
My new ID card should arrive
any moment now.

323
00:25:48,880 --> 00:25:51,440
- A certificate of loss?
- I'll bring it.

324
00:25:51,600 --> 00:25:55,440
Tomorrow, please.
I'll draw up a temporary contract.

325
00:25:56,480 --> 00:25:58,840
- Surname?
- Elza... De Vries.

326
00:26:01,680 --> 00:26:03,280
- First name.
- Elza.

327
00:26:05,040 --> 00:26:06,320
Address?

328
00:26:10,320 --> 00:26:11,320
Address?

329
00:26:12,640 --> 00:26:15,160
I'll come back tomorrow,
when I've got all my papers.

330
00:26:15,320 --> 00:26:17,640
Yes, that might be best.

331
00:26:22,560 --> 00:26:23,720
Miss De Vries.

332
00:26:28,120 --> 00:26:30,040
Trousers are a garment for men.

333
00:26:31,760 --> 00:26:32,760
In-house rules?

334
00:26:32,920 --> 00:26:35,000
No, that's just how it is.

335
00:26:41,400 --> 00:26:44,120
Can listeners hear on the radio
if I'm in a dress or trousers?

336
00:26:50,400 --> 00:26:52,160
Robert Meulemans.

337
00:27:05,920 --> 00:27:06,840
Yes?

338
00:27:10,440 --> 00:27:11,960
Yes, come in.

339
00:28:05,880 --> 00:28:07,120
What's going on here?

340
00:28:36,880 --> 00:28:38,200
Fresh blood...

341
00:28:39,360 --> 00:28:40,360
Hello.

342
00:28:41,400 --> 00:28:42,400
Hi.

343
00:28:43,160 --> 00:28:44,080
Hello.

344
00:28:50,760 --> 00:28:52,040
- Denize.
- Elza.

345
00:28:57,200 --> 00:28:59,920
His name is Charles Lindbergh.

346
00:29:00,080 --> 00:29:02,440
He is no older than 25.

347
00:29:02,600 --> 00:29:05,320
His grandfather came from Sweden.
He is American.

348
00:29:06,320 --> 00:29:10,040
He flies at a speed
of over 170 km per hour.

349
00:29:10,200 --> 00:29:14,000
His aircraft is called
'Spirit of St. Louis'.

350
00:29:14,160 --> 00:29:16,400
Climb into your plane,
Charles Lindbergh.

351
00:29:16,560 --> 00:29:19,000
Climb in and save Europe.

352
00:29:19,160 --> 00:29:20,960
They are expecting you there.

353
00:29:24,040 --> 00:29:25,400
I am the fog.

354
00:29:25,560 --> 00:29:27,720
He who ventures out to sea,
must reckon with me.

355
00:29:27,880 --> 00:29:32,640
For millennia, no one was seen
flying in these skies.

356
00:29:32,920 --> 00:29:36,200
But now, I have been pierced
by a mysterious bird.

357
00:29:36,360 --> 00:29:38,480
So, I ask myself: who are you?

358
00:29:38,640 --> 00:29:40,160
I am many.

359
00:29:40,320 --> 00:29:44,120
I am the power of all the men
who built this machine for me.

360
00:29:44,400 --> 00:29:46,280
So, challenge me.
I am not afraid of you.

361
00:29:46,440 --> 00:29:49,480
Not yet,
because it is still light outside.

362
00:29:49,640 --> 00:29:52,040
Because you still know
where right and left are.

363
00:29:53,240 --> 00:29:54,880
- Dries. Dries?
- Yes?

364
00:29:55,040 --> 00:29:57,200
The snowstorm is fine,

365
00:29:57,360 --> 00:29:59,920
but the fog should be played
by a woman.

366
00:30:00,080 --> 00:30:02,680
The fog requires

367
00:30:02,840 --> 00:30:06,440
a feminine mystique, subtlety.

368
00:30:07,080 --> 00:30:10,000
But wait until the dark night falls.

369
00:30:11,040 --> 00:30:12,400
And nothing more.

370
00:30:13,520 --> 00:30:14,720
I am the fog.

371
00:30:14,880 --> 00:30:16,560
Oh Ida...

372
00:30:16,720 --> 00:30:19,520
You are undoubtedly
the perfect fog.

373
00:30:19,680 --> 00:30:21,200
Yes, maybe.

374
00:30:21,360 --> 00:30:23,320
Hubert, you alright with that?

375
00:30:23,480 --> 00:30:24,800
Everything for art.

376
00:30:24,960 --> 00:30:27,960
But I do wonder if it's wise,
at this moment in time,

377
00:30:28,120 --> 00:30:30,160
to put on a radio play
about Lindbergh.

378
00:30:30,320 --> 00:30:32,480
You have something
against Lindbergh?

379
00:30:32,640 --> 00:30:36,240
Everyone knows that our American hero
is a friend of the Nazis.

380
00:30:36,400 --> 00:30:40,000
So what? He's the best pilot,
the Nazis have the best planes.

381
00:30:40,160 --> 00:30:42,480
Come on, guys.
This is entertainment.

382
00:30:42,640 --> 00:30:46,320
Please, people.
This is not a political radio play.

383
00:30:46,480 --> 00:30:50,760
It's about the greatness of technology.

384
00:30:50,920 --> 00:30:55,680
How technology brings people together,
connects them.

385
00:30:55,840 --> 00:30:58,200
The first flight
across the ocean.

386
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:00,200
What does that represent?

387
00:31:01,160 --> 00:31:04,200
Can you imagine it?
An airplane, what is that?

388
00:31:04,360 --> 00:31:08,880
It is a hand that we are extending
to the future.

389
00:31:09,560 --> 00:31:13,040
This radio play, dear people,
conveys an optimistic message.

390
00:31:13,200 --> 00:31:16,200
Namely, that man is capable
of great things

391
00:31:16,360 --> 00:31:18,200
thanks to technology.

392
00:31:18,360 --> 00:31:19,360
There!

393
00:31:19,520 --> 00:31:22,480
And actually, Ida, I think you're right:

394
00:31:22,640 --> 00:31:25,800
the fog could use
a little feminine mystery.

395
00:31:26,480 --> 00:31:28,280
So, yes,
could you play fog...

396
00:31:28,440 --> 00:31:30,600
- Elza?
- Elza?

397
00:31:33,560 --> 00:31:35,640
Yes, I don't know if that...

398
00:31:35,800 --> 00:31:37,120
Perfect. It's decided.

399
00:31:37,280 --> 00:31:39,200
So, Elza, you'll be
'Fog' and 'Snowstorm'.

400
00:31:39,360 --> 00:31:41,200
Fons is 'Lindbergh'.

401
00:31:41,360 --> 00:31:43,520
Denize,
'Audience' and 'America'.

402
00:31:43,680 --> 00:31:44,880
Ida, you'll be 'Rain'.

403
00:31:45,680 --> 00:31:47,800
Lowie, 'Reporter' and 'Europe'.

404
00:31:47,960 --> 00:31:50,640
And Hubert will be
the 'Airplane Engine'.

405
00:31:52,080 --> 00:31:54,600
Just to tell you
that I don't have a plane.

406
00:31:54,880 --> 00:31:55,840
Excuse me?

407
00:31:56,320 --> 00:31:58,520
No plane.
I ordered one from the BBC,

408
00:31:58,800 --> 00:32:00,920
<i>but they delivered carpenter tools .</i>

409
00:32:01,080 --> 00:32:04,240
- Carpenter tools?
- Please, people!

410
00:32:07,280 --> 00:32:10,760
Mr Polak, the broadcast
is tomorrow evening.

411
00:32:10,920 --> 00:32:13,320
So, you have 24 hours
to bring me a plane.

412
00:32:13,480 --> 00:32:14,680
Come up with something.

413
00:32:18,520 --> 00:32:21,080
OK, let's start again from:

414
00:32:21,960 --> 00:32:23,720
"He who ventures out to sea."

415
00:32:25,280 --> 00:32:27,800
Three, two, one...

416
00:32:27,960 --> 00:32:29,200
Action, Elza!

417
00:32:29,360 --> 00:32:33,280
He who ventures out to sea,
must reckon with me.

418
00:32:33,440 --> 00:32:34,880
For millennia, no one was...

419
00:32:35,040 --> 00:32:38,920
<i>BBC, wood plane.
Take one.</i>

420
00:32:39,080 --> 00:32:42,120
But now, I have been pierced
by a mysterious bird.

421
00:32:42,280 --> 00:32:47,080
So, I ask myself: who are you?

422
00:32:51,640 --> 00:32:53,200
- Thank you all.
- See you tomorrow.

423
00:32:53,360 --> 00:32:55,600
Ida really brought that rain to life.

424
00:32:55,760 --> 00:32:58,760
Drinks on the square,
for those who want...

425
00:34:02,400 --> 00:34:04,240
Polak knows you're using his equipment?

426
00:34:06,920 --> 00:34:09,160
Put everything back where it was.

427
00:34:09,320 --> 00:34:12,160
<i>That bell, yesterday,
in Arsenic and Apple Tart ,</i>

428
00:34:12,320 --> 00:34:13,840
it was the bell of an ice-cream van.

429
00:34:14,960 --> 00:34:17,520
To indicate the inspector
is entering a shop,

430
00:34:17,680 --> 00:34:19,400
why not use a shop bell?

431
00:34:19,560 --> 00:34:22,160
- Polak knows his craft.
- Why not let us hear it?

432
00:34:23,680 --> 00:34:26,520
He creates sound like they did
ten years ago, this is 1940.

433
00:34:27,440 --> 00:34:29,800
Big speech for someone
we've not heard a word from.

434
00:34:29,960 --> 00:34:33,000
Sound is too important
to be treated casually.

435
00:34:33,800 --> 00:34:35,320
Every sound is unique.

436
00:34:36,040 --> 00:34:39,280
If you broadcast it on the radio
and send it into the ether,

437
00:34:39,440 --> 00:34:41,560
and that radio wave escapes Earth,

438
00:34:41,720 --> 00:34:44,400
it will continue
to float through space for eternity.

439
00:34:45,240 --> 00:34:48,200
- Up there, no one's losing sleep over it.
- I am.

440
00:34:52,360 --> 00:34:53,720
You think you can do better?

441
00:34:54,960 --> 00:34:56,000
Yes.

442
00:34:58,000 --> 00:35:00,440
If you think you can do better,
let us hear it.

443
00:35:05,240 --> 00:35:06,200
See you tomorrow.

444
00:35:12,720 --> 00:35:16,720
It's a great destination for you.
Your girls have Austrian passports.

445
00:35:16,880 --> 00:35:19,200
As war refugees,
they automatically get a visa.

446
00:35:19,880 --> 00:35:21,000
- Hello.
- Elza...

447
00:35:21,280 --> 00:35:22,400
Where were you?

448
00:35:22,800 --> 00:35:24,520
In town, out for a walk.

449
00:35:27,560 --> 00:35:29,440
Elza is an obsessive walker.

450
00:35:29,600 --> 00:35:31,680
If it were up to her,
she'd be out all day.

451
00:35:31,960 --> 00:35:34,720
If we don't tie her down in the morning,
she's gone.

452
00:35:34,880 --> 00:35:36,600
The spitting image of your mother.

453
00:35:38,160 --> 00:35:40,680
Mr and Mrs Sarphati
are leaving for Argentina

454
00:35:40,840 --> 00:35:44,040
- but they're not taking their radio.
- Yes, I did warn my wife.

455
00:35:44,320 --> 00:35:46,440
That radio is too expensive
and too big.

456
00:35:46,600 --> 00:35:48,480
Only buy things
that fit in a suitcase.

457
00:35:49,800 --> 00:35:52,280
Your mum would be so proud
of you working in radio.

458
00:35:52,440 --> 00:35:54,920
Elza's not working in radio anymore.
Thank God.

459
00:35:56,560 --> 00:36:00,080
- When are you leaving?
- Friday. Train to Le Havre.

460
00:36:00,240 --> 00:36:02,360
Saturday, we board the ship
for Buenos Aires.

461
00:36:02,520 --> 00:36:05,800
- If you want tickets, you must hurry.
- I'll think about it.

462
00:36:06,640 --> 00:36:08,720
In Tierra del Fuego,
southern Argentina,

463
00:36:08,880 --> 00:36:11,680
the wind blows so hard
that trees grow horizontally.

464
00:36:21,080 --> 00:36:23,600
<i>The German violence
in Poland, Denmark and Norway</i>

465
00:36:23,760 --> 00:36:26,200
<i>shows that Hitler is a man of action...</i>

466
00:36:26,360 --> 00:36:27,320
In the little well?

467
00:36:27,600 --> 00:36:30,640
<i>... and that he will not hesitate
to violate Belgian neutrality.</i>

468
00:36:32,320 --> 00:36:33,840
In the well too, Berre?

469
00:36:35,440 --> 00:36:38,000
<i>Germany's warlike language
exerts an intimidating force</i>

470
00:36:38,160 --> 00:36:40,680
<i>on the spirit of the Belgian citizens.</i>

471
00:36:41,960 --> 00:36:43,600
<i>The Minister of the Interior urges</i>

472
00:36:43,880 --> 00:36:46,160
<i>the population to continue their daily
routines.</i>

473
00:36:54,000 --> 00:36:57,280
<i>Belgium is strong, our army is ready.</i>

474
00:36:59,040 --> 00:37:01,400
That German moustache
will be in Brussels soon.

475
00:37:01,560 --> 00:37:03,760
Hitler has no business here.
He knows better.

476
00:37:56,600 --> 00:37:58,280
"For now, he feels strong,

477
00:37:58,440 --> 00:38:01,600
but he doesn't know
who he's up against."

478
00:38:05,200 --> 00:38:08,680
It'll be funny when Dad hears you
on national radio tomorrow.

479
00:38:12,520 --> 00:38:13,680
"Who are you really?

480
00:38:14,720 --> 00:38:15,800
Who are you?"

481
00:38:30,320 --> 00:38:31,520
Come on...

482
00:39:16,280 --> 00:39:17,520
Wait a moment, André.

483
00:39:17,680 --> 00:39:19,320
Wait a moment.

484
00:39:19,760 --> 00:39:20,960
His sandwiches.

485
00:39:22,240 --> 00:39:24,280
He must have left really early.

486
00:39:24,440 --> 00:39:26,880
Not even a goodbye,
a kiss or anything.

487
00:39:27,160 --> 00:39:29,680
- Poor lad.
- His racket keeps us awake all night,

488
00:39:29,840 --> 00:39:32,240
we find the dismantled coffee grinder
in his room,

489
00:39:32,400 --> 00:39:35,280
and now, I have
to deliver his sandwiches to him.

490
00:39:35,640 --> 00:39:38,760
Poor lad doesn't know any better.
Show some compassion.

491
00:39:43,120 --> 00:39:44,760
Drive safely.

492
00:39:56,440 --> 00:39:58,200
Good morning, ladies and gentlemen.

493
00:39:58,360 --> 00:40:01,200
Let's start by adopting
a gymnast's stance.

494
00:40:01,360 --> 00:40:03,720
Raise your heel,
bring your left arm forward.

495
00:40:03,880 --> 00:40:05,000
Now.

496
00:40:05,720 --> 00:40:09,320
Make sure the arm and torso
form a 135-degree angle.

497
00:40:10,280 --> 00:40:13,200
Let the heels fall
and bring down the arm.

498
00:40:15,680 --> 00:40:18,680
Alternately, bring the left,
then the right arm forward,

499
00:40:18,960 --> 00:40:21,160
at that same 135-degree angle.

500
00:40:21,880 --> 00:40:22,880
Start.

501
00:40:23,240 --> 00:40:25,840
Raise and lower.

502
00:40:26,000 --> 00:40:28,360
Raise and lower.

503
00:40:28,520 --> 00:40:31,960
Mr Polak, you know
how much we value your work

504
00:40:32,120 --> 00:40:33,680
but you must pull yourself together.

505
00:40:34,920 --> 00:40:37,640
There have been too many complaints
about your gloomy

506
00:40:37,800 --> 00:40:40,000
and overly negative attitude.

507
00:40:42,680 --> 00:40:43,880
This is a free train ticket.

508
00:40:44,040 --> 00:40:45,720
Third class. Jerusalem.

509
00:40:45,880 --> 00:40:49,320
One way. It says it clearly.
"One way, never to return."

510
00:40:51,120 --> 00:40:52,040
Where's it from?

511
00:40:52,200 --> 00:40:55,360
There's one in my letterbox every day.
Every single day.

512
00:40:59,200 --> 00:41:00,520
You can keep it.

513
00:41:02,760 --> 00:41:06,640
Pierre, I know you're being persecuted
by different groups.

514
00:41:06,800 --> 00:41:09,360
- And I feel for you, but...
- They're coming.

515
00:41:09,520 --> 00:41:11,640
What am I saying?
They're already here.

516
00:41:12,280 --> 00:41:13,560
It's swarming with fascists.

517
00:41:14,840 --> 00:41:17,480
We can't allow ourselves
to wallow in gloom.

518
00:41:17,640 --> 00:41:20,080
As radio producers
we must continue to believe

519
00:41:20,240 --> 00:41:24,120
that with the help of modern technology,
good will prevail.

520
00:41:26,400 --> 00:41:28,000
I'm counting on no more complaints.

521
00:41:30,160 --> 00:41:31,480
I'm counting on it too.

522
00:41:32,920 --> 00:41:34,080
Glad to hear it.

523
00:41:50,880 --> 00:41:52,280
Thank you for everything.

524
00:41:55,600 --> 00:41:56,560
Come on now...

525
00:41:57,240 --> 00:41:58,240
Energy!

526
00:43:39,240 --> 00:43:40,800
Take good care of that girl.

527
00:44:28,520 --> 00:44:29,440
Come on now!

528
00:44:32,200 --> 00:44:33,160
Energy!

529
00:45:03,880 --> 00:45:04,840
Good morning.

530
00:45:11,560 --> 00:45:12,520
What happened?

531
00:45:36,800 --> 00:45:38,240
It's De Vries, right?

532
00:45:39,280 --> 00:45:40,280
De Vries.

533
00:45:41,640 --> 00:45:42,760
And where are you from?

534
00:45:43,840 --> 00:45:44,800
Antwerp.

535
00:45:47,640 --> 00:45:49,920
And your training,
Royal Theatre Academy?

536
00:45:51,480 --> 00:45:52,400
My mother.

537
00:46:00,040 --> 00:46:01,640
The fog sits in the middle.

538
00:46:04,920 --> 00:46:05,920
Yes...

539
00:46:46,960 --> 00:46:48,360
Dear friends.

540
00:46:49,200 --> 00:46:53,000
We'll never be able to make sense
of Polak's desperate act.

541
00:46:54,240 --> 00:46:55,800
Let us remember him as a kind man.

542
00:46:57,440 --> 00:46:58,440
A pioneer in his field.

543
00:47:01,480 --> 00:47:02,680
There is no solace.

544
00:47:03,600 --> 00:47:04,560
Only sadness.

545
00:47:05,640 --> 00:47:07,520
But we must go on.

546
00:47:07,680 --> 00:47:10,920
Even in this sombre moment,
we must remain committed to our task.

547
00:47:12,200 --> 00:47:15,280
Our radio signal is
the heartbeat of society.

548
00:47:15,560 --> 00:47:16,960
It must never be interrupted.

549
00:47:17,920 --> 00:47:20,640
Never!
Not even for this tragedy.

550
00:47:21,640 --> 00:47:24,800
This medium is beautiful,
but unforgiving.

551
00:47:24,960 --> 00:47:28,560
Two million listeners count on us.

552
00:47:28,920 --> 00:47:30,040
Yes?

553
00:47:31,280 --> 00:47:33,160
Tonight you go on the air.

554
00:47:33,320 --> 00:47:36,360
As planned, with a new radio play.

555
00:47:36,520 --> 00:47:39,440
At Mr Van Dijck's request,
sound effects will, for now,

556
00:47:39,600 --> 00:47:40,880
be handed over to the intern.

557
00:47:41,800 --> 00:47:43,280
That is,

558
00:47:43,440 --> 00:47:46,640
as long as Uncle André's
new wife's nephew agrees?

559
00:47:46,800 --> 00:47:47,960
Berre.

560
00:47:50,600 --> 00:47:51,680
His name is Berre.

561
00:47:54,680 --> 00:47:55,600
Berre.

562
00:47:57,560 --> 00:48:00,920
So, Director Van Rooy, himself,
designated you?

563
00:48:02,880 --> 00:48:06,360
And he said you could use my moped
for tonight's radio play?

564
00:48:07,200 --> 00:48:09,800
Did he also say
how I was to get home?

565
00:48:10,720 --> 00:48:11,760
By tram.

566
00:48:20,400 --> 00:48:22,720
I'm leaving it with you
until tomorrow morning.

567
00:48:26,160 --> 00:48:29,040
Oh yes,
Aunt Gabby made you sandwiches.

568
00:48:29,600 --> 00:48:31,440
They're on my desk, waiting for you.

569
00:48:35,920 --> 00:48:39,840
And that's why I think it's important
that we dedicate tonight's performance

570
00:48:40,000 --> 00:48:41,520
to Polak.

571
00:48:41,680 --> 00:48:43,520
I'm counting on all of you for that.

572
00:48:43,680 --> 00:48:44,680
Sir?

573
00:48:45,800 --> 00:48:48,840
Isn't Mrs Ida a better choice
to play Fog and Snowstorm?

574
00:48:50,600 --> 00:48:53,680
This is my first radio play
and it's being broadcast live.

575
00:48:53,840 --> 00:48:56,440
Maybe I can just watch tonight
and then next time...

576
00:48:56,600 --> 00:48:57,680
Too much time lost.

577
00:48:57,840 --> 00:48:59,880
If you have any doubts or questions,

578
00:49:00,040 --> 00:49:02,040
Ida will help you.
Yes, Ida?

579
00:49:03,800 --> 00:49:05,200
I, the fog,

580
00:49:05,720 --> 00:49:07,800
I wonder: who are you?

581
00:49:08,560 --> 00:49:09,640
Who are you?

582
00:49:09,800 --> 00:49:11,680
I'm not afraid of you.

583
00:49:11,840 --> 00:49:13,280
You understand?

584
00:49:13,440 --> 00:49:14,960
You'll do great, Elza.

585
00:49:15,840 --> 00:49:17,880
Absolutely. Let's get started.

586
00:49:33,680 --> 00:49:35,720
From the Brussels Flagey building,

587
00:49:35,880 --> 00:49:38,480
the Drama Company,
led by Dries Van Dijck, presents

588
00:49:38,640 --> 00:49:40,960
<i>Bertolt Brecht's Lindbergh's Flight.</i>

589
00:49:41,120 --> 00:49:43,600
Performed by: Fons Belloy,
Hubert Van Vaerenberg,

590
00:49:43,760 --> 00:49:46,040
Ida Verdoodt,
Denize Hoovaerts, Lowie Depuydt

591
00:49:46,320 --> 00:49:48,280
and, for the first time, Elza De Vries.

592
00:49:48,440 --> 00:49:52,080
Technician: Jozef Vandesande.
Sound effects: Berre Meulemans.

593
00:50:37,400 --> 00:50:39,000
TREE / FOG

594
00:50:57,440 --> 00:50:58,440
Elza...

595
00:51:04,280 --> 00:51:05,480
I am the fog.

596
00:51:06,360 --> 00:51:07,640
He who ventures out to sea,

597
00:51:07,800 --> 00:51:09,800
<i>must reckon with me.</i>

598
00:51:10,680 --> 00:51:13,280
<i>For millennia, no one was seen
flying in these skies.</i>

599
00:51:14,440 --> 00:51:16,800
But now, I have been pierced
by a mysterious bird.

600
00:51:18,320 --> 00:51:19,840
For now, he feels strong.

601
00:51:20,000 --> 00:51:21,800
But he does not know
who he is dealing with.

602
00:51:23,600 --> 00:51:25,240
Who are you really?

603
00:51:25,760 --> 00:51:27,080
Who are you?

604
00:51:29,600 --> 00:51:30,880
I am many.

605
00:51:31,880 --> 00:51:35,000
I am the power of all the men
who built this machine for me.

606
00:51:36,480 --> 00:51:37,880
So, challenge me.

607
00:51:39,040 --> 00:51:41,440
- I am not afraid of you.
- Not yet.

608
00:51:42,120 --> 00:51:44,080
Because it is still light outside.

609
00:51:44,240 --> 00:51:46,520
Because you still know
where right and left are.

610
00:52:00,240 --> 00:52:01,840
SNOWSTORM

611
00:52:17,520 --> 00:52:19,280
I am the snowstorm.

612
00:52:19,440 --> 00:52:21,840
For an hour now,
a man has been within me.

613
00:52:22,000 --> 00:52:25,760
A man in a machine, first high above,
then far below, close to the water.

614
00:52:25,920 --> 00:52:29,600
For an hour, I've hurled him
at the water, at the skies above.

615
00:52:29,760 --> 00:52:32,720
He has nothing to hold on to,
but he does not go under.

616
00:52:32,880 --> 00:52:35,000
He falls upwards,
he rises downwards.

617
00:52:35,160 --> 00:52:38,280
For hours, he's been unable
to see the moon, nor even his own hand.

618
00:52:38,440 --> 00:52:40,080
But he does not...

619
00:52:40,240 --> 00:52:41,800
<i>But he does not go under.</i>

620
00:52:42,080 --> 00:52:44,520
<i>I have packed his device with ice
to make it heavy</i>

621
00:52:44,680 --> 00:52:46,080
<i>and pull it downward.</i>

622
00:52:46,240 --> 00:52:48,480
<i>But the ice falls off
and he does not go under.</i>

623
00:52:48,760 --> 00:52:50,680
<i>He does not go under.</i>

624
00:53:14,120 --> 00:53:17,240
For centuries,
everything fell from above to below.

625
00:53:17,520 --> 00:53:18,440
Except the birds.

626
00:53:18,600 --> 00:53:21,640
On ancient murals
we never found drawings

627
00:53:21,800 --> 00:53:23,520
of humans flying in the sky.

628
00:53:24,440 --> 00:53:27,680
<i>But now we have succeeded.</i>

629
00:53:27,840 --> 00:53:29,880
At the end of this second millennium,

630
00:53:30,040 --> 00:53:32,360
we have triumphed
over the laws of nature.

631
00:53:32,520 --> 00:53:33,880
The future belongs

632
00:53:34,040 --> 00:53:36,560
to technology and human ingenuity.

633
00:53:36,720 --> 00:53:38,160
Hurrah!

634
00:54:02,240 --> 00:54:03,200
We're off air.

635
00:54:03,360 --> 00:54:04,560
Thank you all!

636
00:54:05,520 --> 00:54:07,560
Yes, thank you. Truly.
Fantastic.

637
00:54:07,720 --> 00:54:09,920
Bravo.
Very good.

638
00:54:10,080 --> 00:54:11,920
Lowie, wow!

639
00:54:12,080 --> 00:54:15,040
Really very good.
Congratulations Fons.

640
00:54:15,840 --> 00:54:17,440
Everyone, thank you!

641
00:54:17,600 --> 00:54:19,000
Hubert, well done.

642
00:54:20,480 --> 00:54:22,760
- Bravo!
- Hats off.

643
00:54:45,560 --> 00:54:47,360
They've gone for a drink,
on the square.

644
00:54:48,920 --> 00:54:50,240
Shall we join them?

645
00:54:52,720 --> 00:54:54,440
There's something I have to do.

646
00:54:56,400 --> 00:54:58,600
- We can walk together?
- I'm taking the tram.

647
00:55:00,400 --> 00:55:02,600
But I have a thing to do first.
See you tomorrow?

648
00:55:03,760 --> 00:55:04,680
Yes.

649
00:55:27,400 --> 00:55:28,480
Well done.

650
00:56:01,400 --> 00:56:03,800
That fog sound, what was that?

651
00:56:04,960 --> 00:56:07,880
The sound of a tree
sucking water up from the ground.

652
00:56:08,040 --> 00:56:11,360
A primeval sound
that existed before any animals.

653
00:56:12,800 --> 00:56:14,440
So, before anyone could hear it?

654
00:56:15,680 --> 00:56:17,880
But, if no one can hear it,
does it exist?

655
00:56:19,400 --> 00:56:21,600
You think trees can't hear?

656
00:56:22,480 --> 00:56:23,920
That, I don't know.

657
00:56:24,960 --> 00:56:26,560
Bye, Berre.
See you tomorrow.

658
00:56:48,040 --> 00:56:49,600
André's already gone to bed.

659
00:56:50,960 --> 00:56:53,280
I kept your dinner warm, Berre.
Come, sit down.

660
00:56:58,040 --> 00:56:59,720
That was so beautiful.

661
00:57:00,840 --> 00:57:02,760
That Lindbergh and his airplane.

662
00:57:05,080 --> 00:57:08,400
I found it very moving.
I can't stop thinking about it.

663
00:57:11,440 --> 00:57:14,720
That man's child
was kidnapped and killed.

664
00:57:48,120 --> 00:57:49,120
Enough salt?

665
00:58:01,360 --> 00:58:03,640
You have such beautiful green eyes.

666
00:58:14,400 --> 00:58:15,680
Elza De Vries.

667
00:58:20,680 --> 00:58:24,520
You think that changing 'Friedmann'
to 'De Vries' solves our problems?

668
00:58:28,960 --> 00:58:30,640
I don't want to flee anymore.

669
00:58:30,800 --> 00:58:33,360
You want to wait for the Nazis
to come and get us?

670
00:58:33,520 --> 00:58:35,120
We're safe here, aren't we?

671
00:58:36,440 --> 00:58:39,440
Belgium accepts more refugees
than anywhere in Europe.

672
00:58:39,600 --> 00:58:41,400
Belgium did accept more refugees.

673
00:58:41,560 --> 00:58:45,240
- But there's panic here too now.
- The king's mother is helping refugees.

674
00:58:58,800 --> 00:59:01,600
"The number of Jews
who fraudulently entered Belgium

675
00:59:01,760 --> 00:59:03,600
"is estimated at 30,000.

676
00:59:03,760 --> 00:59:07,360
"The government must deal strictly
with these suspicious elements,

677
00:59:07,520 --> 00:59:10,600
"mostly foreigners
or recently naturalised Belgians.

678
00:59:10,760 --> 00:59:14,240
"Most of these aliens become
agents of subversive propaganda."

679
00:59:14,400 --> 00:59:15,720
You know who wrote that?

680
00:59:16,880 --> 00:59:19,800
The son of the mother
of the King of Belgium. Leopold III.

681
00:59:22,000 --> 00:59:25,320
For him, you're a suspicious element
we must deal with strictly.

682
00:59:29,280 --> 00:59:33,000
The Dutch queen just vetoed
a reception camp for Jewish refugees

683
00:59:33,160 --> 00:59:35,800
because it was
too close to her country residence.

684
00:59:36,840 --> 00:59:38,360
Twelve kilometres.

685
00:59:39,720 --> 00:59:41,720
I wouldn't trust those royals.

686
00:59:41,880 --> 00:59:45,120
I'll apply for residency.
Then I won't be a refugee anymore.

687
00:59:46,000 --> 00:59:50,040
- You've got Belgian papers too...
- Necessary to practise law in Antwerp.

688
00:59:51,000 --> 00:59:53,040
But you know
I was disbarred last year

689
00:59:53,200 --> 00:59:54,280
because I'm Jewish.

690
00:59:56,160 --> 00:59:57,600
Don't you see, Elza?

691
00:59:58,480 --> 01:00:01,520
It's not just the Nazis
we have to be wary of.

692
01:00:01,680 --> 01:00:03,720
Danger lurks here in Belgium too.

693
01:00:05,800 --> 01:00:09,720
My Belgian passport is precisely
why I'm in more danger than you are.

694
01:00:17,480 --> 01:00:19,720
Anonymity is a precious commodity.

695
01:00:21,160 --> 01:00:22,160
Don't throw it away.

696
01:00:25,320 --> 01:00:27,040
If you apply for residency,

697
01:00:27,200 --> 01:00:29,720
your name will go
on the Belgian authorities' lists.

698
01:00:29,880 --> 01:00:32,880
They'll know where to find you,
be able to deport you.

699
01:00:33,040 --> 01:00:34,280
To Germany, for example.

700
01:00:34,840 --> 01:00:35,760
But that's fine,

701
01:00:35,920 --> 01:00:39,560
the Belgian Justice Minister believes
we Jews are safe in Germany.

702
01:00:39,720 --> 01:00:42,440
What do you suggest?
We keep running?

703
01:00:42,600 --> 01:00:43,960
No, for the last time,

704
01:00:44,120 --> 01:00:47,040
go to a country where they don't
see every refugee as a spy.

705
01:00:56,080 --> 01:00:58,720
If we hadn't fled Vienna,
Mami might still be alive.

706
01:01:02,320 --> 01:01:06,280
Your mum wouldn't stop performing,
even when it became too dangerous.

707
01:01:06,440 --> 01:01:10,040
She craved the spotlight
when it was time to seek the shadows.

708
01:01:11,320 --> 01:01:13,200
Just like you're doing now,
on the radio.

709
01:01:14,640 --> 01:01:16,240
You're putting us in danger.

710
01:01:23,640 --> 01:01:26,640
Tomorrow I'm arranging
visas and tickets to Argentina.

711
01:01:28,800 --> 01:01:31,040
From now on, be less conspicuous.

712
01:01:31,200 --> 01:01:32,400
No trousers anymore.

713
01:01:34,000 --> 01:01:35,480
And no more radio!

714
01:01:43,520 --> 01:01:45,640
Good morning, madam.
Please...

715
01:01:45,800 --> 01:01:49,200
Can you help me?
I'm looking for a dentist.

716
01:01:49,760 --> 01:01:52,920
I'm looking for a dentist.

717
01:02:20,200 --> 01:02:21,520
ELZA PART 1

718
01:03:14,960 --> 01:03:18,160
Hello, madam.
Registration office, please?

719
01:03:18,320 --> 01:03:19,640
Second floor.

720
01:03:39,800 --> 01:03:42,000
That's fine, lad,
I'm good from here.

721
01:03:42,160 --> 01:03:45,320
<i>Dear listeners, war has broken out
in the Low Countries.</i>

722
01:03:45,480 --> 01:03:49,480
<i>A musical war, that is.
Welcome to The Battle of the Bands .</i>

723
01:03:50,440 --> 01:03:51,360
Mrs De Vries.

724
01:03:52,920 --> 01:03:56,000
- You didn't bring your papers yesterday?
- Indeed.

725
01:03:56,160 --> 01:03:58,520
<i>In studio 4, our own Stan Brenders</i>

726
01:03:58,680 --> 01:04:00,840
<i>and his big band,
ready to give their all.</i>

727
01:04:01,000 --> 01:04:02,720
<i>First, our Dutch radio colleagues</i>

728
01:04:02,880 --> 01:04:05,480
<i>present the Dutch band, The Ramblers,</i>

729
01:04:05,640 --> 01:04:08,360
<i>and their song Who is Loesje?</i>

730
01:06:17,800 --> 01:06:20,320
Standing at the altar
in Antwerp Cathedral,

731
01:06:20,480 --> 01:06:22,240
I am overcome by doubt.

732
01:06:22,400 --> 01:06:25,680
My future husband
is 37 years older than me.

733
01:06:25,840 --> 01:06:27,120
He could be my father.

734
01:06:27,400 --> 01:06:29,040
Will I be able to handle this marriage?

735
01:06:29,200 --> 01:06:30,200
Great.

736
01:06:30,360 --> 01:06:33,000
Berre, next, the organ music,
followed by Lowie.

737
01:06:33,160 --> 01:06:34,160
Go ahead.

738
01:06:34,320 --> 01:06:38,800
Pieter Paul Rubens, do you take
Hélène Fourment as your wife?

739
01:06:38,960 --> 01:06:40,000
Yes.

740
01:06:40,160 --> 01:06:43,640
Hélène Fourment, do you take
Pieter Paul Rubens as your husband?

741
01:06:43,800 --> 01:06:44,800
Yes.

742
01:06:44,960 --> 01:06:47,240
Place the rings on each other's fingers.

743
01:06:47,400 --> 01:06:50,560
In the name of the Father,
the Son and the Holy Ghost.

744
01:06:50,720 --> 01:06:53,840
Pieter Paul Rubens,
you may kiss the bride.

745
01:06:56,440 --> 01:06:57,560
Yes, that's enough.

746
01:06:57,720 --> 01:06:59,720
Come on, Hubert, let the girl be.

747
01:07:00,360 --> 01:07:01,280
Enough, Hubert...

748
01:07:01,440 --> 01:07:02,800
Hubert!

749
01:07:09,600 --> 01:07:10,880
Unusual approach.

750
01:07:12,120 --> 01:07:15,360
Yes, Stanislavski, comrade.
The new acting method.

751
01:07:33,680 --> 01:07:34,600
Berre.

752
01:07:35,480 --> 01:07:37,360
Director Van Rooy wants to see you.

753
01:07:39,080 --> 01:07:40,000
Now.

754
01:07:55,560 --> 01:07:56,600
They're actors.

755
01:07:57,360 --> 01:07:58,720
You mustn't forget that.

756
01:08:00,080 --> 01:08:02,360
It's all just a game to those men.

757
01:08:02,520 --> 01:08:04,400
You have so much talent.
Really.

758
01:08:05,320 --> 01:08:07,520
Don't let their nonsense
ruin everything.

759
01:08:09,640 --> 01:08:11,800
Tomorrow, on air, yes?

760
01:08:13,240 --> 01:08:14,520
You'll do great.

761
01:08:21,960 --> 01:08:23,400
<i>Ministry of Defence confirms</i>

762
01:08:23,560 --> 01:08:26,080
increased troop movements
at the German border.

763
01:08:26,760 --> 01:08:30,600
We can therefore assume
that the Germans will soon...

764
01:08:31,600 --> 01:08:34,720
It's fine. Mr Meulemans has been
invited by me, personally.

765
01:08:34,880 --> 01:08:37,400
He is the late Mr Polak's intern.

766
01:08:37,560 --> 01:08:40,600
Was, I mean.
As of now, he is his successor.

767
01:08:42,400 --> 01:08:44,680
Your collaboration with National Radio

768
01:08:44,840 --> 01:08:46,840
commits you to secrecy.
Can you do that?

769
01:08:47,000 --> 01:08:49,160
- I'm good at keeping quiet.
- Good to hear.

770
01:08:49,320 --> 01:08:52,920
You'll take over Polak's duties,
also his role in our emergency team.

771
01:08:53,080 --> 01:08:54,640
Take a seat.

772
01:08:54,800 --> 01:08:58,760
With the help of Emergency Services,
we're prepping for an invasion.

773
01:08:58,920 --> 01:09:01,280
If the Germans do invade,

774
01:09:01,440 --> 01:09:03,560
this building will be a prime target.

775
01:09:03,720 --> 01:09:06,960
Well, we will make sure
they find nothing but an empty shell.

776
01:09:07,120 --> 01:09:10,920
All the equipment in this building
must be made inoperable.

777
01:09:11,080 --> 01:09:12,000
Mr Meulemans,

778
01:09:12,160 --> 01:09:14,720
you will help dismantle
the Radio Play studios.

779
01:09:14,880 --> 01:09:19,080
At the same time, in a secret location,
we will start a new radio station

780
01:09:19,360 --> 01:09:21,800
which will take over
the transmission signal,

781
01:09:21,960 --> 01:09:24,760
the moment the main transmitter
here is shut down.

782
01:09:25,720 --> 01:09:28,360
In the Radio Play studio is
an emergency transmitter.

783
01:09:28,520 --> 01:09:31,320
You will ensure
the technical service staff

784
01:09:31,480 --> 01:09:33,000
have access to that studio,

785
01:09:33,160 --> 01:09:35,880
so that they can dismantle
that last transmitter.

786
01:09:36,760 --> 01:09:37,680
Everything clear?

787
01:09:38,480 --> 01:09:39,400
Good.

788
01:09:39,560 --> 01:09:40,760
One last thing...

789
01:09:52,640 --> 01:09:55,320
EMERGENCY TRANSMITTER

790
01:10:01,360 --> 01:10:05,320
<i>Whoever is not brave
cannot stand with us</i>

791
01:10:05,480 --> 01:10:09,240
<i>He who dares not, must perish</i>

792
01:10:09,760 --> 01:10:12,680
<i>Should the hard battle come...</i>

793
01:10:12,840 --> 01:10:16,600
<i>We are ready!</i>

794
01:10:22,160 --> 01:10:25,600
<i>The hour will come</i>

795
01:10:26,600 --> 01:10:29,120
<i>Red-hot as fire</i>

796
01:10:29,880 --> 01:10:34,000
<i>The enemy will stand grimly before us</i>

797
01:10:34,160 --> 01:10:37,320
<i>And the hour of a Dietsch victory
will strike</i>

798
01:10:38,440 --> 01:10:41,840
<i>Should the hard battle come...</i>

799
01:10:42,000 --> 01:10:45,480
<i>We are ready!</i>

800
01:10:51,040 --> 01:10:54,240
<i>Loyal unto death</i>

801
01:10:55,720 --> 01:10:58,680
<i>Dietschland will grow...</i>

802
01:10:58,840 --> 01:11:02,440
<i>Goodbye, my dear little officer</i>

803
01:11:04,520 --> 01:11:10,160
<i>Adieu, and don't forget me</i>

804
01:11:12,280 --> 01:11:16,320
<i>Goodbye, my dear little officer</i>

805
01:11:18,480 --> 01:11:21,960
<i>Adieu, may luck be with you...</i>

806
01:11:24,160 --> 01:11:26,520
<i>The Dietsch folk will rise</i>

807
01:11:27,040 --> 01:11:31,000
<i>If tomorrow you fall
And I remain alone</i>

808
01:11:31,160 --> 01:11:35,280
<i>My friend, stay faithful
I will fight for two</i>

809
01:11:35,440 --> 01:11:38,840
<i>Should the hard battle come...</i>

810
01:11:39,000 --> 01:11:43,200
<i>We are ready!</i>

811
01:12:11,280 --> 01:12:13,160
Berre, guess what just happened?

812
01:12:13,320 --> 01:12:16,800
André was riding home
and his front wheel came off.

813
01:12:16,960 --> 01:12:18,840
The cops brought it here.

814
01:12:19,000 --> 01:12:21,280
He has to stay in the hospital
until tomorrow.

815
01:12:21,440 --> 01:12:22,920
How could that happen?

816
01:12:23,080 --> 01:12:25,480
There's something in the air
that I don't like.

817
01:12:27,440 --> 01:12:30,680
Berre lad, you will put my glasses back
in my cupboard?

818
01:12:30,840 --> 01:12:32,600
When you're done?

819
01:12:43,280 --> 01:12:45,880
ELZA, PART 2

820
01:12:56,600 --> 01:12:57,800
The tickets.

821
01:13:00,720 --> 01:13:03,920
We leave the day after tomorrow.
At 6 am.

822
01:13:05,880 --> 01:13:08,200
Decide if you're coming with us
or staying.

823
01:13:09,560 --> 01:13:13,320
I won't let you put Rifka and me
in danger any longer.

824
01:13:50,920 --> 01:13:53,840
Miss De Vries, I must insist
you go by the secretariat.

825
01:13:54,000 --> 01:13:56,320
I'm already late
and we're about to go on air.

826
01:13:56,480 --> 01:13:57,720
Miss De Vries...

827
01:14:00,040 --> 01:14:01,080
Come with me, please.

828
01:14:09,280 --> 01:14:10,560
Miss De Vries.

829
01:14:17,840 --> 01:14:18,960
Miss De Vries.

830
01:14:19,120 --> 01:14:22,240
We've been informed
that you don't have valid papers.

831
01:14:24,640 --> 01:14:26,840
You may know
that the Ministry of Justice

832
01:14:27,000 --> 01:14:28,400
has requested lists be made

833
01:14:28,560 --> 01:14:31,280
of possible spies
or Nazi sympathisers who may be

834
01:14:31,440 --> 01:14:33,600
preparing for a German invasion,

835
01:14:33,760 --> 01:14:36,640
- but also...
- You think I'm spying for the Germans?

836
01:14:38,480 --> 01:14:40,320
But also of illegal refugees.

837
01:14:43,880 --> 01:14:46,560
Your real name and address are known.

838
01:14:48,080 --> 01:14:50,120
Someone has put you on the list.

839
01:14:54,000 --> 01:14:58,800
We are obliged to follow the rules
laid down by the political authorities.

840
01:14:58,960 --> 01:15:03,640
If, by tomorrow afternoon, you haven't
brought in your identity papers,

841
01:15:04,360 --> 01:15:07,720
we will have no choice
but to terminate our cooperation.

842
01:15:23,120 --> 01:15:24,480
Miss Friedmann.

843
01:15:28,160 --> 01:15:29,360
Be careful.

844
01:15:53,160 --> 01:15:54,360
You denounced me.

845
01:15:57,520 --> 01:15:58,800
I'm antisemitic.

846
01:16:00,880 --> 01:16:02,480
I want nothing to do with you people.

847
01:16:03,720 --> 01:16:05,920
I see you as parasites,
I can't help it.

848
01:16:11,000 --> 01:16:12,760
You should flee.

849
01:16:27,080 --> 01:16:28,600
<i>Morning, Denize, welcome.</i>

850
01:16:28,760 --> 01:16:30,080
<i>Good morning.</i>

851
01:16:30,360 --> 01:16:31,920
<i>Hello, Denize.</i>

852
01:16:33,240 --> 01:16:35,560
<i>- Shall we start?
- Yes, just a moment.</i>

853
01:16:35,720 --> 01:16:38,080
- Wow, guys, what a bright sun.
- Nice, isn't it?

854
01:16:38,800 --> 01:16:40,480
Good morning, Elza.

855
01:16:42,200 --> 01:16:43,920
I think it's going to be a great day.

856
01:16:45,400 --> 01:16:46,320
Here.

857
01:16:46,480 --> 01:16:49,200
Berre, what's the purpose
of all these squares?

858
01:16:49,360 --> 01:16:51,080
They're for the acoustics.

859
01:16:51,800 --> 01:16:54,600
The distance between
the actors and the mics must vary

860
01:16:54,760 --> 01:16:57,280
to recreate the echo
inside Antwerp Cathedral.

861
01:16:58,280 --> 01:16:59,320
So...

862
01:16:59,480 --> 01:17:02,040
Lowie, Antwerp mayor, number three.

863
01:17:02,600 --> 01:17:05,080
Denize and Ida, noblewomen,
numbers eight and six.

864
01:17:05,240 --> 01:17:08,000
Guys, do as he asks,
there's not much time.

865
01:17:08,160 --> 01:17:10,120
Dries, I was a bishop, wasn't I?

866
01:17:11,000 --> 01:17:12,880
Yes, and now
you're the mayor of Antwerp.

867
01:17:13,040 --> 01:17:14,800
Yes, but mayor?

868
01:17:14,960 --> 01:17:16,920
Being mayor isn't hard, Lowie.

869
01:17:17,080 --> 01:17:18,320
Come on.

870
01:17:19,440 --> 01:17:21,600
Rubens, number one.

871
01:17:23,960 --> 01:17:25,360
Helena, number five.

872
01:17:26,960 --> 01:17:29,000
And Bishop van Malderen, number four.

873
01:17:34,920 --> 01:17:35,920
Let's go.

874
01:17:42,640 --> 01:17:43,800
Great. Here we go!

875
01:17:44,480 --> 01:17:48,280
Helena and Rubens
are walking along the Scheldt.

876
01:17:48,440 --> 01:17:49,640
Elza?

877
01:17:49,840 --> 01:17:52,960
Ready?
Three, two, one...

878
01:17:54,640 --> 01:17:57,080
<i>From the Flagey building in Brussels,</i>

879
01:17:57,240 --> 01:17:59,120
<i>the NIR Drama Company
presents</i>

880
01:17:59,280 --> 01:18:01,840
<i>the radio play,
The Marriage of Rubens.</i>

881
01:18:02,000 --> 01:18:04,560
<i>Starring Hubert Van Vaerenberg,</i>

882
01:18:04,720 --> 01:18:07,280
<i>Elza De Vries and Fons Belloy.</i>

883
01:18:07,440 --> 01:18:08,960
<i>The year is 1630.</i>

884
01:18:09,680 --> 01:18:11,640
<i>Helena Fourment meets Pieter Paul Rubens</i>

885
01:18:11,800 --> 01:18:13,760
<i>on the banks of the Scheldt.</i>

886
01:18:16,280 --> 01:18:20,120
Noble Sir, will you tell me
how you paint those masterpieces?

887
01:18:20,280 --> 01:18:21,760
My dear lady, I will tell you,

888
01:18:21,920 --> 01:18:25,320
if you first tell me
from whence your great beauty comes.

889
01:18:25,480 --> 01:18:28,800
My origins, sir, are modest and simple.

890
01:18:28,960 --> 01:18:31,440
<i>But I will not be beguiled
by your flattery.</i>

891
01:18:31,600 --> 01:18:36,160
<i>I sought a girl who would not blush
when she saw me pick up my brush.</i>

892
01:18:36,320 --> 01:18:38,440
<i>I feared pride,
the affliction of nobility.</i>

893
01:18:39,160 --> 01:18:41,600
<i>But you, Helena, you are different.</i>

894
01:18:42,400 --> 01:18:43,320
You are so pure.

895
01:18:43,480 --> 01:18:45,880
And tomorrow,
you will be mine forever.

896
01:18:46,680 --> 01:18:47,880
ORGAN MUSIC

897
01:18:50,560 --> 01:18:54,360
Pieter Paul Rubens,
do you take Helena Fourment

898
01:18:54,520 --> 01:18:55,960
to be your lawful wife?

899
01:18:56,520 --> 01:18:58,880
Yes, I take her as my wife.

900
01:18:59,160 --> 01:19:02,600
Helena Fourment,
do you take Pieter Paul Rubens

901
01:19:02,760 --> 01:19:04,480
as your lawful husband?

902
01:19:07,960 --> 01:19:10,440
Helena Fourment,
do you take Pieter Paul Rubens

903
01:19:10,720 --> 01:19:12,720
as your lawful husband?

904
01:19:13,800 --> 01:19:14,760
Come on, Elza.

905
01:19:28,000 --> 01:19:31,800
Helena Fourment,
do you take Pieter Paul Rubens

906
01:19:31,960 --> 01:19:33,560
as your lawful husband?

907
01:19:55,400 --> 01:19:56,440
Parasites?

908
01:20:00,320 --> 01:20:03,280
What have I ever done
for you to call me a parasite?

909
01:20:07,080 --> 01:20:10,600
I can only be free here
if I stay invisible.

910
01:20:10,760 --> 01:20:11,760
Is that it?

911
01:20:14,720 --> 01:20:16,040
We were driven out of Vienna.

912
01:20:17,120 --> 01:20:19,560
We were no longer welcome in Antwerp,
and now,

913
01:20:19,720 --> 01:20:22,560
<i>in Brussels too,
I have to hide because I'm Jewish?</i>

914
01:20:24,080 --> 01:20:26,120
<i>What gives you the right?</i>

915
01:20:26,280 --> 01:20:30,320
What gives anyone the right to decide
who has to live here, or there,

916
01:20:30,480 --> 01:20:32,080
or who must disappear?

917
01:20:40,560 --> 01:20:42,960
It's all just a game to you.

918
01:20:47,160 --> 01:20:50,760
I'm done playing that game.

919
01:24:15,640 --> 01:24:17,600
I'm working on something for you.

920
01:24:17,760 --> 01:24:19,200
But it's not finished yet.

921
01:24:20,600 --> 01:24:23,560
I can let you hear something,
but it's only a first draft.

922
01:24:48,880 --> 01:24:50,240
Your tree.

923
01:24:51,080 --> 01:24:52,120
Your sound.

924
01:24:54,640 --> 01:24:57,000
In Argentina,
some trees grow horizontally.

925
01:24:57,280 --> 01:25:00,640
- What would they sound like?
- Even warmer. Even more Elza.

926
01:25:02,520 --> 01:25:03,720
I'd like to hear that.

927
01:25:05,000 --> 01:25:06,160
Stand there.

928
01:25:07,320 --> 01:25:08,680
In the middle.

929
01:25:11,520 --> 01:25:12,720
Close your eyes.

930
01:25:21,000 --> 01:25:22,760
Your ears are connected to your brain.

931
01:25:24,280 --> 01:25:26,800
But your skin speaks
directly to your heart.

932
01:25:31,080 --> 01:25:32,440
You can hear with your skin.

933
01:25:33,120 --> 01:25:34,200
Eyes closed.

934
01:25:48,080 --> 01:25:50,120
Coat off, shoes off, dress off.

935
01:25:51,560 --> 01:25:53,880
- Berre, I...
- We need skin.

936
01:25:55,080 --> 01:25:58,040
- You know I'm Jewish, don't you?
- Does that make a difference?

937
01:26:00,080 --> 01:26:01,320
Skin is skin.

938
01:26:04,960 --> 01:26:06,280
Close your eyes again.

939
01:26:06,960 --> 01:26:09,160
The less you see,
the more you feel.

940
01:26:09,640 --> 01:26:10,560
Take off your dress.

941
01:26:16,560 --> 01:26:19,560
Your ears only hear a fraction
of all the sounds around you.

942
01:26:20,200 --> 01:26:23,240
Ultrasonic sounds above 20,000 hertz

943
01:26:23,400 --> 01:26:25,800
and infrasonic vibrations below 6 hertz

944
01:26:25,960 --> 01:26:28,480
are best felt with your bare skin.

945
01:26:29,560 --> 01:26:31,080
Better than with your ears.

946
01:26:46,040 --> 01:26:47,040
Sit down.

947
01:26:48,480 --> 01:26:49,720
On the floor.

948
01:27:09,040 --> 01:27:10,840
Everything is connected.

949
01:27:12,280 --> 01:27:14,120
This room to the house.

950
01:27:14,840 --> 01:27:16,160
The house to the street.

951
01:27:16,440 --> 01:27:18,600
The street to the city.
The city to the Earth.

952
01:27:20,920 --> 01:27:22,880
One big sound box.

953
01:27:24,880 --> 01:27:26,480
Where were you born?

954
01:27:27,080 --> 01:27:28,280
Vienna.

955
01:27:28,440 --> 01:27:30,080
And where did you grow up?

956
01:27:30,240 --> 01:27:34,960
In Vienna, but also in Antwerp
and for a few years, in Berlin.

957
01:27:36,200 --> 01:27:39,720
Wherever my mother sang or
my father worked, that's where we lived.

958
01:27:40,960 --> 01:27:44,680
Your sounds from Berlin
and Vienna and Antwerp,

959
01:27:44,840 --> 01:27:47,240
or wherever you've been,
are vibrations

960
01:27:47,400 --> 01:27:50,680
that have blended with
the Earth's vibrational frequencies.

961
01:27:50,960 --> 01:27:52,600
You'll feel it.
Lie down.

962
01:28:04,240 --> 01:28:06,680
It's only a start, I hardly know you.

963
01:28:07,360 --> 01:28:09,680
There are still too many sounds missing.

964
01:28:10,440 --> 01:28:12,680
You have a lot of fear
and sorrow in you.

965
01:28:13,800 --> 01:28:16,120
But I don't know yet
what that sounds like.

966
01:30:26,880 --> 01:30:28,080
It was beautiful.

967
01:30:29,680 --> 01:30:30,840
It's not finished yet.

968
01:30:31,960 --> 01:30:32,880
No.

969
01:30:33,560 --> 01:30:36,080
You still need to find out
what the sound of my sorrow is.

970
01:30:41,320 --> 01:30:42,240
Listen.

971
01:30:54,520 --> 01:30:55,600
The fear

972
01:30:56,520 --> 01:30:58,600
of being left behind, alone.

973
01:31:01,600 --> 01:31:03,000
That's what it sounds like.

974
01:31:08,280 --> 01:31:10,440
At 6 am,
we're leaving for South America.

975
01:31:12,880 --> 01:31:15,080
Come with us, if you want.
You have papers.

976
01:31:20,640 --> 01:31:21,800
The choice is yours.

977
01:31:23,560 --> 01:31:25,600
Six o'clock at my place,
or never again.

978
01:33:42,800 --> 01:33:47,320
<i>Over night, the German Air Force
and Army launched an attack</i>

979
01:33:47,480 --> 01:33:49,680
<i>on Belgian airfields.</i>

980
01:33:50,360 --> 01:33:52,520
- Berre, we have to take cover.
- I have to go.

981
01:33:52,680 --> 01:33:54,960
Now? But the air-raid siren?

982
01:33:55,120 --> 01:33:57,160
- Where are you going?
- Argentina.

983
01:33:57,880 --> 01:34:00,640
Argentina?
What are you saying?

984
01:34:00,800 --> 01:34:03,480
I'm meeting Elza.
We're leaving at 6 am.

985
01:34:07,880 --> 01:34:09,000
Thanks so much, Auntie.

986
01:34:16,080 --> 01:34:17,440
But, lad...?

987
01:34:18,480 --> 01:34:19,880
Come, Gabby.

988
01:34:48,480 --> 01:34:49,880
Friedmann family?

989
01:34:50,040 --> 01:34:51,160
I don't know...

990
01:34:52,720 --> 01:34:54,000
I don't know...

991
01:35:25,440 --> 01:35:27,040
<i>Dear listeners,</i>

992
01:35:27,200 --> 01:35:30,320
<i>this is an important announcement
to the population.</i>

993
01:35:30,840 --> 01:35:33,440
Last night, the German armed forces

994
01:35:33,600 --> 01:35:37,200
launched an attack on Belgium,
the Netherlands and Luxembourg.

995
01:35:37,360 --> 01:35:41,280
The German Air Force launched
numerous attacks in Belgian airspace.

996
01:35:42,120 --> 01:35:46,480
<i>Since 5 am, German airplanes
have been flying over our country.</i>

997
01:35:46,640 --> 01:35:49,440
<i>A state of emergency has been
declared in Brussels.</i>

998
01:35:50,000 --> 01:35:53,440
Meulemans, you have to take
the equipment outside, not inside.

999
01:35:53,600 --> 01:35:56,920
<i>The period of greatest danger has begun.</i>

1000
01:35:57,080 --> 01:36:00,840
<i>We ask the population to strictly obey
all governmental orders.</i>

1001
01:36:02,040 --> 01:36:05,400
<i>The government is arresting suspects
throughout the country.</i>

1002
01:36:05,560 --> 01:36:09,560
<i>Fascists, communists and all subversives
will be arrested on the spot</i>

1003
01:36:09,720 --> 01:36:12,520
<i>- to defend internal security.
- Morning.</i>

1004
01:36:15,200 --> 01:36:17,720
Your letters of termination.
Van Vaerenberg.

1005
01:36:20,360 --> 01:36:23,240
Actors are apparently redundant
in times of war.

1006
01:36:24,200 --> 01:36:25,440
Verdoodt.

1007
01:36:26,520 --> 01:36:27,760
Depuydt.

1008
01:36:30,240 --> 01:36:31,200
Isn't Fons here?

1009
01:36:32,480 --> 01:36:35,240
Our government's busy arresting
enemies of the state,

1010
01:36:35,400 --> 01:36:37,280
so with his fascist party member's card,

1011
01:36:37,440 --> 01:36:40,520
he's going to hit the jackpot.

1012
01:36:40,680 --> 01:36:42,040
Hovaerts.

1013
01:36:43,120 --> 01:36:45,360
Maybe someone denounced him?

1014
01:36:47,720 --> 01:36:52,120
<i>Civilians are requested to remove
all Pacha Chicorei advertisements.</i>

1015
01:36:52,280 --> 01:36:55,520
<i>The back of these adverts
contain information for spies.</i>

1016
01:36:55,680 --> 01:36:58,480
Hired the day before yesterday
and already fired today.

1017
01:36:59,280 --> 01:37:00,760
That must be a record.

1018
01:37:02,160 --> 01:37:03,920
Where do they take the arrested?

1019
01:37:06,480 --> 01:37:07,960
They've taken her.

1020
01:37:11,360 --> 01:37:13,080
Barracks, prisons...

1021
01:37:14,320 --> 01:37:15,960
It could be anywhere.

1022
01:37:17,040 --> 01:37:18,800
They won't hold her for long.

1023
01:38:32,600 --> 01:38:34,000
Rachel Vos.

1024
01:38:37,600 --> 01:38:38,640
Elza.

1025
01:38:40,320 --> 01:38:41,400
Friedmann.

1026
01:38:48,720 --> 01:38:50,200
Rifka Friedmann.

1027
01:39:02,520 --> 01:39:03,680
<i>Le Vernet.</i>

1028
01:39:04,120 --> 01:39:05,160
<i>Full.</i>

1029
01:39:06,160 --> 01:39:07,120
<i>Abbeville.</i>

1030
01:39:07,280 --> 01:39:08,560
<i>Full.</i>

1031
01:39:09,960 --> 01:39:12,600
<i>Saint-Cyprien.
There's room here. Here!</i>

1032
01:39:16,040 --> 01:39:17,000
Get in.

1033
01:39:20,160 --> 01:39:22,360
- No dawdling...
- Wait.

1034
01:39:22,520 --> 01:39:24,360
- Come on.
- No, but wait. No, wait!

1035
01:39:24,520 --> 01:39:26,280
You can't put Jews in with us...

1036
01:39:26,440 --> 01:39:29,920
There must be separate carriages
for the Jews, surely?

1037
01:39:30,080 --> 01:39:31,720
This must be a mistake.

1038
01:39:31,880 --> 01:39:34,280
I am Fons Belloy. Actor.
Actor at the NIR.

1039
01:39:34,440 --> 01:39:37,560
The National Radio.
From the radio plays.

1040
01:39:37,720 --> 01:39:39,400
You know my voice.
Listen to it!

1041
01:39:39,560 --> 01:39:42,760
You know my voice!
From radio plays!

1042
01:39:42,920 --> 01:39:45,960
I'm an actor!
I was Scrooge. Charles Dickens.

1043
01:39:46,120 --> 01:39:48,560
<i>Sharp Mackie
in The Threepenny Opera.</i>

1044
01:39:48,720 --> 01:39:50,520
I was Hamlet! I played Hamlet!

1045
01:39:50,680 --> 01:39:52,000
To be or not to be!

1046
01:39:52,160 --> 01:39:54,560
That was me! I'm an actor!

1047
01:40:41,520 --> 01:40:44,440
<i>Ladies and gentlemen,
please stay tuned.</i>

1048
01:40:45,280 --> 01:40:46,920
<i>In a few minutes,</i>

1049
01:40:47,080 --> 01:40:50,400
<i>your Prime Minister, Mr Pierlot,
will address the nation.</i>

1050
01:40:50,560 --> 01:40:53,120
<i>Ladies and gentlemen,
in a few moments,</i>

1051
01:40:53,400 --> 01:40:58,400
<i>Mr Pierlot, your Prime Minister,
will address the nation</i>

1052
01:40:58,560 --> 01:40:59,520
<i>Following that,</i>

1053
01:40:59,680 --> 01:41:04,800
<i>the esteemed Mr Vanderpoorten,
Minister of the Interior, will speak.</i>

1054
01:41:14,480 --> 01:41:17,120
Three, two, one...

1055
01:41:17,280 --> 01:41:18,280
And switch over.

1056
01:41:23,200 --> 01:41:24,920
And shut it down.

1057
01:41:44,960 --> 01:41:46,480
Dear listeners.

1058
01:41:47,080 --> 01:41:48,680
From now on, the broadcasts

1059
01:41:48,840 --> 01:41:51,320
<i>of the National Radio Institute</i>

1060
01:41:51,480 --> 01:41:54,200
<i>will be taken over
by our mobile studio.</i>

1061
01:41:54,360 --> 01:41:57,320
<i>This is Prime Minister
Hubert Pierlot speaking.</i>

1062
01:42:00,840 --> 01:42:01,960
Fellow countrymen.

1063
01:42:02,560 --> 01:42:03,560
Have faith.

1064
01:42:04,280 --> 01:42:05,640
<i>In these dark times,</i>

1065
01:42:05,800 --> 01:42:08,040
<i>it is vitally important
that we stand together.</i>

1066
01:42:09,000 --> 01:42:11,800
<i>The enemy has invaded our country.</i>

1067
01:42:11,960 --> 01:42:14,320
<i>But our troops are putting up
an heroic resistance.</i>

1068
01:42:22,880 --> 01:42:24,360
ELZA, PART 3

1069
01:44:08,280 --> 01:44:09,600
Ladies and gentlemen.

1070
01:44:09,760 --> 01:44:12,640
We will now hear a speech
by Mr Arthur Vanderpoorten,

1071
01:44:12,800 --> 01:44:14,800
Minister of the Interior.

1072
01:44:23,880 --> 01:44:25,080
In different locations,

1073
01:44:25,240 --> 01:44:27,960
enemy paratroopers have landed...

1074
01:45:23,480 --> 01:45:25,520
Elza, Elza, Elza.

1075
01:45:38,760 --> 01:45:40,000
<i>Six o'clock at your place.</i>

1076
01:45:45,760 --> 01:45:48,280
<i>How my heart longs for you...</i>

1077
01:45:56,520 --> 01:45:58,280
Isn't that our Berre?

1078
01:46:00,600 --> 01:46:03,240
<i>Your words are so soft and sweet.</i>